{
  "id": "Compound::DB08860 - Disease::DOID:3393",
  "verbalization": "Pitavastatin, a HMG-CoA reductase inhibitor, is implicated in the pathogenesis of Coronary Artery Disease through a series of mechanistic links. The evidence suggests that Pitavastatin may contribute to the development of Immune-mediated Necrotising Myopathy, a condition that is associated with Coronary Artery Disease. This relationship is supported by the shared pathway involving HMG-CoA reductase inhibition, which is a well-established mechanism in lipid metabolism and a key target for statins. The observed causal relationships between Pitavastatin and Coronary Artery Disease are mediated through the indirect pathway of Rosuvastatin, Lovastatin, and Simvastatin, all of which are also HMG-CoA reductase inhibitors. The low scores in the paths indicate a moderate level of confidence in the causal relationships, but the consistent involvement of HMG-CoA reductase inhibition as a common mechanism strengthens the hypothesis. The integration of these pathways suggests that Pitavastatin may exert a modulatory effect on lipid metabolism, potentially contributing to the development of Coronary Artery Disease through a complex interplay of immune and metabolic pathways.",
